generally speaking you never want to be  in a small boat in the middle of the  ocean with two very opinionated airline  pilots particularly if a rain storm is  coming in and one of those pilots is  your mother but this happened to me  recently and it got me thinking besides  bringing back to driving to all kinds of  new levels it reveals something about  commercial pilots that I've always known  they have each other's backs and they  make great teams growing up my brother  and I were always very proud of our  mothers career as a pilot we loved  hearing her wild stories and we always  got a kick out of hearing her voice over  the loudspeaker before landing but for  us flying was a sort of exotic foreign  part of her life especially as I got  older I thought her career has very  little to do with mine but a day on the  boat and other days I realized that  that's not altogether true they're  elements of aviation that a broader  audience can learn from specifically  pilots have unlocked the code to  teamwork at the rest of us need imagine  for a moment the copilot has controls  over the 767 as it approaches Newark and  suddenly the wind changes direction and  the hail is hitting the windshield so  heavily that the flight crew is actually  shouting at each other to get through  the landing checklist on top of all this  it looks like they may need to make a  last-minute switch to a shorter runway  to the outside observer the space seem  like chaos but to everyone involved  everything is as it should be the pilot  the copilot the flight attendants that  the company dispatchers are all working  together like one instrument what would  you do with that kind of teeth think  about that for every flight you've ever  taken for every flight that left San  Francisco Airport this morning one of  thousands of pilots showed up to work  may have never  the copilot before may never meet that  person again but for the next eight or  nine hours well you're in the back  working or watching rom-coms or whatever  we do on airplanes they became a highly  functioning team the kind of team that  deals with last-minute windstorms and  engine failures and landing decisions  what would you do with that kind of team  now I know at this point I may have  totally lost you you may be thinking  flying is way too far out of an analogy  to be relevant to my career its  unionized the promotions are based on  seniority not performance and it's so  dangerous the 180 people in the back of  that plane are way more important than  any spreadsheet that I ever built but  bear with me I used to think that way  too but I think there are elements of  aviation that fuel this kind of  camaraderie that the rest of us can  learn from and before diving into what  those are there's something else that I  should tell you about my mother in 1973  when she applied to American Airlines  she could not imagine doing anything  else her brother was a pilot her father  was a pilot from what I can tell she cut  more high school and she attended to  become a pilot if it's not already here  she is much cooler than I am but there  was one problem American had never hired  a woman before in fact no major airline  had and it turns out that she's quite  stubborn which is unlucky for me and  lucky for her in this situation and she  got hired but when she showed up for  work she sometimes struggled to fit in  literally they had to redesign the  pilot's uniform and of course this  cockpit culture that was very developed  an established had to shift a little bit  with some being more receptive than  others and the reason this is relevant  is because despite all of these seeming  obstacles the camaraderie she had always  associated with flying persisted even  though her flying community had  ballooned from the handful of people at  pompano beach  South Florida Airport to the thousands  of people at any major hub everyone had  each other's back I think that is  extraordinary so today I'd like to  suggest three things that I think create  this environment that maybe we can adapt  in our own the first is simple is  proximity when yahoo decided they no  longer were in allowing people to work  remotely there was a lot of buzz but  behind that decision was a very basic  assumption that to work well people need  to work together they need to see each  other in person frequently and sometimes  without interruption and commercial  aviation takes that to an extreme if you  think about the cockpit you might also  ask when was the last time that you  spent eight or nine hours alone in a  closet-sized room with just your boss  and your direct report with no meal  breaks and certainly no internet surfing  and you just stayed there for a long  time no wonder these people have bonds  and I know that sounds silly but over  the course of the few months that I've  been thinking about this I've had the  chance to ask many of you about your  experiences with camaraderie in the  workplace and a theme emerged often if  work extended late into the night  sometimes appear or a boss would you sit  with you and sometimes they would help  you and sometimes they would do their  own thing but they're just being there  created a sense of motivation that  lasted for years to come proximity the  second idea is a concept there's a  combination of shared expertise with  distinct roles at American every six  months every pilot goes down to Dallas  for intense and rigorous training  simulator training emergency training  you name it and one consequence besides  being relief to all of the passengers is  everyone knows what everyone else knows  everyone shares the latest expertise my  mom has described it as cut from the  same cloth you know what I know  I know what you know and we trust each  other at the same time everyone in the  cockpit has a very distinct role down to  a checklist everyone is distinct and  everyone is necessary just like the  parts of an engine the combination of  these two things means that everyone has  a context and understanding but also a  dependency that creates these  astonishing teams and the third idea is  purpose in the cockpit the purpose is  very clear get your passengers from  point A to point B safely and as quickly  as possible and the process may be  complicated and the roadblocks might be  unexpected but the outcome is clear well  general general McChrystal came to the  GSB earlier this fall he shared an idea  a similar idea called shared  consciousness the role of the manager is  not only to articulate the purpose but  to make sure that everyone gets behind  it purpose I know that the analogy to  flying implies some degree of extreme  extreme danger extreme weather extreme  camaraderie but I think that this  teamwork plays out most authentically in  smaller moments and one of those moments  happened on one of my mother's first  layovers in Chicago you're approaching  the airport and as I mentioned at the  time there were no other female pilots  so a good shorthand for the pilots  lounge was male crew only so in  anticipation of this someone pulled her  aside and said Bonnie just so you know  this is a situation you may want to  steer clear and so she did I mentioned  that she was stubborn but she's also  very polite and she was certainly not  there to create a stir so she was going  to shuffle off so they land and they're  approaching the lounge and she sees it  sure enough it says male crew only but  as they get closer she sees that someone  written something underneath and it now  says milk who only and Bonnie too I'm  sure that she played it cool but this  was obviously very exciting and  triumphant for her but I think it also  signals the astonishing way the  camaraderie is at play at one of the  largest such a large organization so  today I talked about three ideas that I  think create this environment proximity  shared with distinct and purpose so I  asked you if you could capture even a  slice of this camaraderie for yourselves  imagine what we could do thank you
 

